WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
For our guiding case, u equals three,
Node six gave an exact identity:

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.460
E of omega divided by c of omega
equals E of one divided by c of one.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.540
Here omega is a primitive
cube root of unity.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.920
Now use the all ones
congruence from Node five.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.840
Modulo one plus i, E has the
same coefficients as J. We will

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.700
show that these two equal normalized
quantities would have different residues.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.840
Recall J of P equals one plus
P, through P to the eighth.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.360
At one, all nine terms become one,
and their sum is nine. At omega,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.220
group the nine terms into three copies
of one plus omega plus omega squared.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.810
Each copy has complex sum zero,
so J of omega is exactly zero.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.880
Consequently, modulo one plus
i, E of one has residue one,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.900
while E of omega has residue
zero. The denominators remain

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.680
invertible in the reduction
ring, as established in Node six.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.540
In particular, c of one is positive or
negative three, which has residue one.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.990
Thus E of one divided by
c of one has residue one.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.060
E of omega divided by c
of omega has residue zero.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.800
But these normalized quantities are equal.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.060
Zero and one are distinct in our
reduction ring: this is a contradiction.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.060
It rules out u equal to three,

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.440
so no circulant Hadamard matrix
of order thirty six exists.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.600
When u has several distinct prime
divisors, there are more evaluations.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:37.340
For general odd u greater than one, let I
be the set of distinct primes dividing u.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.420
For each subset S, use
zeta sub S, the product

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.380
of the corresponding prime roots of
unity. The empty subset gives one.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.680
Node six gives exact identities
between alternating products.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.420
Expand those products, use
the sign divisibilities,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:56.940
and cancel in characteristic zero
before reducing modulo one plus i.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:02.540
This yields an alternating sum: sum
over S of negative one to the size of S,

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:07.720
times E of zeta sub S
divided by c of zeta sub S.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.250
Call this sum script
S. Its residue is zero.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.620
The smaller nodes give the exact
expansions and cancellations.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.880
With several primes, these product
identities replace the two input picture;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.040
we do not assume a common rotation
between every pair of inputs.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.820
The final node combines this
vanishing sum with the all

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.080
ones congruence to rule out
every odd u greater than one.
